# game-rule-counterintuitive-edge-cases-must-be-applied-exactly

Both chess and Dartman contain rules that contradict common intuition (a pinned piece can still give check; a deflector cannot rotate; Dartman pieces can shoot each other; a king is never actually captured; a king cannot escape to a square still controlled by the opponent). These edge cases recurred across multiple skills in this session and must be applied with full precision rather than by intuitive default. When reasoning about legal moves, captures, or win conditions in either game, explicitly check against each known counterintuitive rule rather than relying on general pattern-matching.

## Procedures
- Before evaluating legality of any move or outcome, enumerate the relevant counterintuitive rules for that game.
- For chess: check pinned-piece-can-check, king-never-captured, king-escape-restrictions, and drawn-endgame-positions rules explicitly.
- For Dartman: check deflector-orientation-fixed, no-opponent-deflector-stopping, and mutual-dartman-shooting rules explicitly.
- Do not infer rule behavior from analogies to other games or common sense; use the stated rule verbatim.

## Conventions
- When explaining a position or ruling, cite the specific counterintuitive rule being applied rather than just stating the outcome.
- If a rule seems to produce a surprising result, surface the specific rule as the reason rather than softening the conclusion.

